WHY THE NEGATIVE ARM IS A SEPARATE BINARY, not a build flag: the mechanism is a build-tagged file pair keyed
on goexperiment.burroughsspawn, so dropping the experiment would also remove the spawn door and the arms
would differ in two variables. The stub keeps the door and removes only the notification.

THE DIFFERENCE IS CHECKABLE IN THE ARTIFACT (standing property 13), not merely in the build command:

  $ wasm-tools print c2_<arm>.wasm | awk '/\(func \$syscall\.Read /{f=1} f{print} f&&/^  \)$/{exit}' \
      | grep -c 'entersyscallblock\|exitsyscall'
  c2_present.wasm -> 2
  c2_absent.wasm  -> 0

REGISTERED READINGS (phase4/clause2-recon.md, "The discriminating pair") and what this pair produces:
  mechanism present -> sibling B advanced, 10 of 10     REPRODUCED (rebuild): 10 of 10
  mechanism absent  -> sibling B delta 0, every run     REPRODUCED (rebuild): deltaB = 0, 3 of 3
  Sibling A advanced in both arms on every run (observation 1), asserted per run so a zero for B cannot be
  vacuous.

THE MAGNITUDES CHANGED WITH THE REBUILD, AND THE DISCRIMINATOR DID NOT. The guest's closing report runs
only when `main` regains a P. In the PRESENT arm both Ps are held by spinners once the P is handed off, so
`main` reports after they finish, and the deltas read ~the whole bound (deltaA ~8.0M, deltaB ~8.0M), not
the old ~1.0M-per-hold, which was the host's sample taken inside the hold. In the ABSENT arm `main` keeps
its P, returns and reports at once (deltaA ~2.4M, deltaB = 0). B still advances only if the P was handed
off, so B > 0 against B = 0 discriminates as before. The interval is wider and the question is the same.

RACE-FREE UNDER A DETECTOR THAT CAN SEE IT (#979): the present arm re-encoded with
`(memory 1024 1024 shared)`, so its memory is on the Go heap. 0 race reports in 3 -race runs. The old
host-sampled guest, re-encoded the same way, gave 3 reports in 1 run.

SPIN BOUND: 8,000,000, and the constraint is an ORDERING rather than a duration: sibling A must NOT finish
its spin during the 400ms hold. If it does, it frees its own P, sibling B runs on that P, and the negative
arm shows B advancing for a reason unrelated to the handoff.

  (Measured on the superseded 2026-09-26 build, whose host sampled the counters inside the hold. The
  ordering constraint is the guest's and carries over unchanged.)
  Measured at 2,000,000 (a first attempt to make CI cheaper):
    ABSENT arm -> deltaA=2000000 exactly, deltaB~350000, 3 of 3.  Discrimination DESTROYED.
  Measured at 8,000,000:
    ABSENT arm -> deltaA~1.14M of 8M (A still running), deltaB=0, 3 of 3.  Correct.

A smaller bound is not the safer choice here; it is what breaks the negative arm. CI's `-race` cost is
handled by scaling the BOUND (see racebound_race_test.go), not by shrinking the guest: under -race the
interpreter is ~9x slower, so A consumes even less of its bound inside the hold, and `main` then waits ~25s
for A to finish and free a P.
